1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Look at the back of a big

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:04,481
bulldozer and you will see something strange.

3
00:00:04,661 --> 00:00:06,178
A giant steel claw,

4
00:00:06,178 --> 00:00:09,131
hanging there like a scorpion's tail.

5
00:00:09,311 --> 00:00:11,336
Most people think the blade at the

6
00:00:11,336 --> 00:00:12,945
front is the whole machine.

7
00:00:13,125 --> 00:00:14,562
But on hard ground,

8
00:00:14,562 --> 00:00:17,513
the blade is useless without that claw.

9
00:00:17,693 --> 00:00:20,681
Today, eleven parts of a bulldozer,

10
00:00:20,681 --> 00:00:22,730
and why each one exists,

11
00:00:22,730 --> 00:00:24,694
starting with the claw.

12
00:00:25,044 --> 00:00:27,320
Bulldozers build roads,

13
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:29,793
airports, dams and mines.

14
00:00:29,973 --> 00:00:33,131
They clear land after disasters and push

15
00:00:33,131 --> 00:00:35,499
rock in quarries all day long.

16
00:00:35,679 --> 00:00:37,976
The biggest ones weigh more than a

17
00:00:37,976 --> 00:00:40,610
hundred tonnes, and they move dirt that

18
00:00:40,610 --> 00:00:42,772
no human crew could ever move by

19
00:00:42,772 --> 00:00:43,110
hand.

20
00:00:43,290 --> 00:00:45,466
Every part on them is built for

21
00:00:45,466 --> 00:00:47,852
brute force, and for surviving it.

22
00:00:48,202 --> 00:00:51,090
As always, every fact comes from a

23
00:00:51,090 --> 00:00:53,552
named source, machine makers,

24
00:00:53,552 --> 00:00:57,288
engineering references, and museum archives,

25
00:00:57,288 --> 00:00:59,836
all listed in the description.

26
00:01:00,016 --> 00:01:01,827
Let's start at the back.

27
00:01:02,177 --> 00:01:05,004
Why do bulldozers have a giant claw

28
00:01:05,004 --> 00:01:05,973
on the back?

29
00:01:06,153 --> 00:01:07,860
It is called a ripper.

30
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:10,931
A blade can only push loose material.

31
00:01:11,111 --> 00:01:13,328
On hard, packed ground,

32
00:01:13,328 --> 00:01:15,737
frozen soil or soft rock,

33
00:01:15,737 --> 00:01:18,436
it just slides over the top.

34
00:01:18,616 --> 00:01:21,592
So the operator lowers the ripper into

35
00:01:21,592 --> 00:01:23,941
the ground and drives forward,

36
00:01:23,941 --> 00:01:26,681
and the claw tears the ground open,

37
00:01:26,681 --> 00:01:28,090
like a giant plow.

38
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:30,978
Then the dozer turns around and pushes

39
00:01:30,978 --> 00:01:33,471
the broken material with its blade.

40
00:01:33,821 --> 00:01:37,002
Ripping is often cheaper than blasting with

41
00:01:37,002 --> 00:01:37,815
explosives.

42
00:01:37,995 --> 00:01:40,067
In many quarries and mines,

43
00:01:40,067 --> 00:01:42,752
rock that is cracked or layered can

44
00:01:42,752 --> 00:01:45,054
be ripped instead of blown up,

45
00:01:45,054 --> 00:01:46,205
which is safer,

46
00:01:46,205 --> 00:01:48,584
quieter, and needs no drilling.

47
00:01:48,764 --> 00:01:51,089
A big dozer with a single shank

48
00:01:51,089 --> 00:01:53,866
ripper can break rock that would stop

49
00:01:53,866 --> 00:01:55,741
almost any other machine.

50
00:01:56,092 --> 00:01:58,617
The whole weight of the dozer pushes

51
00:01:58,617 --> 00:01:59,740
the ripper down,

52
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:02,616
and hydraulic cylinders adjust its angle.

53
00:02:02,796 --> 00:02:05,834
That is one reason big dozers are

54
00:02:05,834 --> 00:02:06,662
so heavy.

55
00:02:06,842 --> 00:02:09,315
The weight is not a side effect.

56
00:02:09,495 --> 00:02:11,086
It is part of the tool.

57
00:02:11,436 --> 00:02:13,592
Why does the ripper have a small

58
00:02:13,592 --> 00:02:15,209
separate tip at its end?

59
00:02:15,389 --> 00:02:18,512
Because rock grinds steel away fast.

60
00:02:18,692 --> 00:02:21,126
The point that digs into the ground

61
00:02:21,126 --> 00:02:22,447
takes all the wear,

62
00:02:22,447 --> 00:02:24,255
and it can be worn down in

63
00:02:24,255 --> 00:02:25,577
hours in hard rock.

64
00:02:25,757 --> 00:02:26,835
So the shank,

65
00:02:26,835 --> 00:02:27,913
the long arm,

66
00:02:27,913 --> 00:02:30,566
is protected by a removable tip,

67
00:02:30,566 --> 00:02:34,215
and sometimes by a replaceable shield around

68
00:02:34,215 --> 00:02:34,464
it.

69
00:02:34,814 --> 00:02:36,502
When the tip wears out,

70
00:02:36,502 --> 00:02:38,778
a mechanic knocks out a pin and

71
00:02:38,778 --> 00:02:40,247
slides on a new one.

72
00:02:40,427 --> 00:02:43,011
It takes minutes instead of replacing the

73
00:02:43,011 --> 00:02:45,280
whole shank, which can weigh tonnes.

74
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:48,302
The same idea is used on excavator

75
00:02:48,302 --> 00:02:52,147
buckets, with their rows of replaceable teeth.

76
00:02:52,498 --> 00:02:54,740
The tips are made of very hard,

77
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:56,259
wear resistant steel.

78
00:02:56,439 --> 00:02:59,474
Designers balance hardness and toughness,

79
00:02:59,474 --> 00:03:01,695
because a tip that is too hard

80
00:03:01,695 --> 00:03:04,138
can crack when it hits a boulder,

81
00:03:04,138 --> 00:03:06,062
and a tip that is too soft

82
00:03:06,062 --> 00:03:06,877
wears away.

83
00:03:07,057 --> 00:03:09,149
It is a small part doing the

84
00:03:09,149 --> 00:03:11,166
hardest job on the machine.

85
00:03:11,517 --> 00:03:14,876
Why do big Caterpillar dozers have tracks

86
00:03:14,876 --> 00:03:16,760
shaped like a triangle,

87
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:18,645
instead of a flat oval?

88
00:03:18,825 --> 00:03:20,847
Because the drive gear,

89
00:03:20,847 --> 00:03:22,605
called the sprocket,

90
00:03:22,605 --> 00:03:24,187
is lifted high up,

91
00:03:24,187 --> 00:03:25,594
out of the dirt.

92
00:03:25,774 --> 00:03:29,905
Caterpillar introduced this elevated sprocket design in

93
00:03:29,905 --> 00:03:32,008
the late nineteen seventies.

94
00:03:32,188 --> 00:03:34,893
Before that, the sprocket sat low,

95
00:03:34,893 --> 00:03:36,165
at ground level,

96
00:03:36,165 --> 00:03:38,870
where it took every shock from the

97
00:03:38,870 --> 00:03:41,415
ground and was buried in mud and

98
00:03:41,415 --> 00:03:41,813
rock.

99
00:03:42,162 --> 00:03:44,605
Raising it keeps the final drive,

100
00:03:44,605 --> 00:03:47,047
the gears that turn the sprocket,

101
00:03:47,047 --> 00:03:49,637
away from the worst impacts and the

102
00:03:49,637 --> 00:03:50,673
abrasive dirt.

103
00:03:50,853 --> 00:03:53,177
It also makes those parts modular,

104
00:03:53,177 --> 00:03:55,570
so they can be removed and replaced

105
00:03:55,570 --> 00:03:56,391
more easily.

106
00:03:56,571 --> 00:03:58,896
The lower part of the track stays

107
00:03:58,896 --> 00:04:00,869
long and flat on the ground,

108
00:04:00,869 --> 00:04:03,618
to keep the machine stable and pushing.

109
00:04:03,967 --> 00:04:07,543
It became a signature of Caterpillar's track

110
00:04:07,543 --> 00:04:08,681
type tractors.

111
00:04:08,861 --> 00:04:10,627
If you see a dozer with a

112
00:04:10,627 --> 00:04:12,182
triangle shaped track,

113
00:04:12,182 --> 00:04:14,725
you can usually guess the brand from

114
00:04:14,725 --> 00:04:15,502
a distance.

115
00:04:15,682 --> 00:04:19,415
A design choice made for durability became

116
00:04:19,415 --> 00:04:21,104
a visual trademark.

117
00:04:21,453 --> 00:04:24,922
Why is a bulldozer blade curved instead

118
00:04:24,922 --> 00:04:25,633
of flat?

119
00:04:25,813 --> 00:04:28,658
Because a curved blade rolls the material.

120
00:04:28,838 --> 00:04:30,515
As the dozer pushes,

121
00:04:30,515 --> 00:04:33,284
the dirt rises up the curved face

122
00:04:33,284 --> 00:04:34,962
and tumbles forward,

123
00:04:34,962 --> 00:04:36,639
folding over itself.

124
00:04:36,819 --> 00:04:39,781
That rolling action moves the load ahead

125
00:04:39,781 --> 00:04:42,297
of the blade with less effort than

126
00:04:42,297 --> 00:04:44,296
scraping it as a flat pile.

127
00:04:44,646 --> 00:04:47,645
Blades come in different shapes for different

128
00:04:47,645 --> 00:04:47,979
jobs.

129
00:04:48,159 --> 00:04:51,288
A straight blade is good for leveling

130
00:04:51,288 --> 00:04:52,303
and grading.

131
00:04:52,483 --> 00:04:54,483
A U blade has big wings at

132
00:04:54,483 --> 00:04:56,637
the sides, so it can carry a

133
00:04:56,637 --> 00:05:00,252
large load over long distances without spilling

134
00:05:00,252 --> 00:05:00,483
it.

135
00:05:00,663 --> 00:05:03,519
A semi U blade is in between.

136
00:05:03,699 --> 00:05:05,986
The cutting edge at the bottom is

137
00:05:05,986 --> 00:05:08,480
a bolted on strip of hardened steel,

138
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:10,421
replaced when it wears down.

139
00:05:10,770 --> 00:05:13,537
Hydraulic cylinders lift the blade,

140
00:05:13,537 --> 00:05:15,829
tilt it from side to side and

141
00:05:15,829 --> 00:05:18,596
sometimes pitch it forward or back.

142
00:05:18,776 --> 00:05:21,865
Modern dozers can even control the blade

143
00:05:21,865 --> 00:05:23,642
automatically with GPS,

144
00:05:23,642 --> 00:05:26,500
cutting the ground to an exact design

145
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:28,431
within a few centimeters,

146
00:05:28,431 --> 00:05:30,130
without survey stakes.

147
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:33,660
Why do bulldozers use tracks instead of

148
00:05:33,660 --> 00:05:34,231
wheels?

149
00:05:34,411 --> 00:05:37,116
Because pushing is all about grip.

150
00:05:37,296 --> 00:05:39,601
A dozer can only push as hard

151
00:05:39,601 --> 00:05:42,225
as its tracks can grip the ground

152
00:05:42,225 --> 00:05:43,577
before they slip.

153
00:05:43,757 --> 00:05:46,169
Tracks put a long strip of steel

154
00:05:46,169 --> 00:05:47,225
on the ground,

155
00:05:47,225 --> 00:05:50,316
with grousers, little ridges that dig in.

156
00:05:50,496 --> 00:05:53,089
That gives far more traction on soft

157
00:05:53,089 --> 00:05:55,465
or loose ground than tires could.

158
00:05:55,816 --> 00:05:57,917
Tracks also spread the weight.

159
00:05:58,097 --> 00:06:00,565
A heavy dozer can drive across soft

160
00:06:00,565 --> 00:06:02,822
ground where a truck would sink.

161
00:06:03,002 --> 00:06:06,637
Special swamp dozers have extra wide tracks,

162
00:06:06,637 --> 00:06:09,693
so their ground pressure is very low,

163
00:06:09,693 --> 00:06:12,336
and they can work in marshes and

164
00:06:12,336 --> 00:06:13,080
wetlands.

165
00:06:13,430 --> 00:06:14,904
The price is wear.

166
00:06:15,084 --> 00:06:16,467
The steel links,

167
00:06:16,467 --> 00:06:20,442
pins, rollers and sprockets grind against dirt

168
00:06:20,442 --> 00:06:21,911
and rock all day.

169
00:06:22,091 --> 00:06:23,396
On big machines,

170
00:06:23,396 --> 00:06:26,251
the undercarriage can be one of the

171
00:06:26,251 --> 00:06:28,372
biggest maintenance costs.

172
00:06:28,552 --> 00:06:32,022
A track that looks indestructible is actually

173
00:06:32,022 --> 00:06:34,798
slowly wearing out with every meter.

174
00:06:35,148 --> 00:06:38,383
Why is the bulldozer operator's cab built

175
00:06:38,383 --> 00:06:40,593
inside a strong steel frame?

176
00:06:40,773 --> 00:06:44,607
It is called a rollover protective structure,

177
00:06:44,607 --> 00:06:45,289
or ROPS.

178
00:06:45,469 --> 00:06:47,140
Dozers work on slopes,

179
00:06:47,140 --> 00:06:49,266
at the edge of cliffs and on

180
00:06:49,266 --> 00:06:50,101
loose fill.

181
00:06:50,281 --> 00:06:51,761
If one rolls over,

182
00:06:51,761 --> 00:06:54,557
the cab must not collapse onto the

183
00:06:54,557 --> 00:06:55,297
operator.

184
00:06:55,477 --> 00:06:57,918
The frame is tested to carry the

185
00:06:57,918 --> 00:07:01,046
machine's weight and absorb the energy of

186
00:07:01,046 --> 00:07:01,885
a rollover.

187
00:07:02,236 --> 00:07:05,469
It only works if the operator stays

188
00:07:05,469 --> 00:07:08,795
inside, so a seat belt is essential.

189
00:07:08,975 --> 00:07:13,001
Studies of rollover accidents found that operators

190
00:07:13,001 --> 00:07:15,336
who wore seat belts in a ROPS

191
00:07:15,336 --> 00:07:17,027
cab usually survived,

192
00:07:17,027 --> 00:07:19,684
while many who were thrown out or

193
00:07:19,684 --> 00:07:21,456
tried to jump did not.

194
00:07:21,806 --> 00:07:25,114
Many dozers also have a falling object

195
00:07:25,114 --> 00:07:29,381
protective structure, to protect against rocks or

196
00:07:29,381 --> 00:07:31,558
trees falling from above.

197
00:07:31,738 --> 00:07:35,813
Forestry and demolition dozers often get extra

198
00:07:35,813 --> 00:07:37,496
screens and guards.

199
00:07:37,676 --> 00:07:40,115
The cab looks like a small office,

200
00:07:40,115 --> 00:07:42,194
but it is built like a safety

201
00:07:42,194 --> 00:07:42,553
cell.

202
00:07:42,903 --> 00:07:46,029
Why would anyone build a bulldozer weighing

203
00:07:46,029 --> 00:07:48,429
about a hundred and fifty tonnes?

204
00:07:48,609 --> 00:07:50,369
Because in some mines,

205
00:07:50,369 --> 00:07:53,810
a bigger dozer moves material more cheaply.

206
00:07:53,990 --> 00:07:56,681
The Komatsu D five seven five A

207
00:07:56,681 --> 00:08:00,413
is one of the largest production bulldozers

208
00:08:00,413 --> 00:08:01,281
ever made.

209
00:08:01,461 --> 00:08:04,152
Its super dozer version has a blade

210
00:08:04,152 --> 00:08:06,921
more than seven meters wide that can

211
00:08:06,921 --> 00:08:10,227
push about seventy cubic meters of material

212
00:08:10,227 --> 00:08:10,842
at once.

213
00:08:11,192 --> 00:08:13,912
The engine makes more than a thousand

214
00:08:13,912 --> 00:08:14,721
horsepower.

215
00:08:14,901 --> 00:08:16,353
In big coal mines,

216
00:08:16,353 --> 00:08:19,337
the giant dozer can push huge amounts

217
00:08:19,337 --> 00:08:22,401
of overburden, the rock and soil above

218
00:08:22,401 --> 00:08:24,256
the coal, down a slope,

219
00:08:24,256 --> 00:08:26,917
straight into the mined out area.

220
00:08:27,097 --> 00:08:29,651
That can replace trucks for some jobs.

221
00:08:30,002 --> 00:08:32,176
Like giant mining trucks,

222
00:08:32,176 --> 00:08:35,133
it cannot be driven between sites.

223
00:08:35,313 --> 00:08:37,792
It is taken apart and moved on

224
00:08:37,792 --> 00:08:40,271
many trucks, then reassembled.

225
00:08:40,451 --> 00:08:42,476
Caterpillar's D eleven,

226
00:08:42,476 --> 00:08:45,206
its rival, is a common sight in

227
00:08:45,206 --> 00:08:46,616
mines worldwide.

228
00:08:46,796 --> 00:08:49,449
These machines are the heavyweights of moving

229
00:08:49,449 --> 00:08:49,802
earth.

230
00:08:50,153 --> 00:08:52,538
Why do you sometimes see a bulldozer

231
00:08:52,538 --> 00:08:54,924
pushing another machine from behind?

232
00:08:55,104 --> 00:08:57,666
The other machine is a scraper,

233
00:08:57,666 --> 00:09:00,806
a big wheeled earthmover that slices a

234
00:09:00,806 --> 00:09:03,533
thin layer of soil into its bowl.

235
00:09:03,713 --> 00:09:06,006
Scrapers are fast on the road,

236
00:09:06,006 --> 00:09:07,916
but loading is hard work.

237
00:09:08,096 --> 00:09:10,632
So a dozer pushes the scraper from

238
00:09:10,632 --> 00:09:12,273
behind while it loads,

239
00:09:12,273 --> 00:09:14,809
giving it the extra force it needs

240
00:09:14,809 --> 00:09:16,002
to fill quickly.

241
00:09:16,353 --> 00:09:19,971
The dozer uses a special reinforced plate,

242
00:09:19,971 --> 00:09:21,178
or push block,

243
00:09:21,178 --> 00:09:23,849
so it does not damage its blade

244
00:09:23,849 --> 00:09:25,141
or the scraper.

245
00:09:25,321 --> 00:09:28,713
One pusher dozer can serve several scrapers,

246
00:09:28,713 --> 00:09:31,103
loading them one after another.

247
00:09:31,283 --> 00:09:34,234
It is a choreography of machines on

248
00:09:34,234 --> 00:09:36,763
big road and airport projects.

249
00:09:37,113 --> 00:09:40,127
This teamwork is also why dozers are

250
00:09:40,127 --> 00:09:41,466
built so strong.

251
00:09:41,646 --> 00:09:43,988
They push their own load all day,

252
00:09:43,988 --> 00:09:46,685
and then they push other machines too.

253
00:09:46,865 --> 00:09:49,332
The bulldozer is the muscle that the

254
00:09:49,332 --> 00:09:51,114
rest of the site leans on.

255
00:09:51,465 --> 00:09:54,559
Why do some armies use bulldozers covered

256
00:09:54,559 --> 00:09:55,238
in armor?

257
00:09:55,418 --> 00:09:58,968
Because engineers need to clear roads,

258
00:09:58,968 --> 00:10:03,733
fill trenches, remove obstacles and build defenses,

259
00:10:03,733 --> 00:10:05,321
often under fire.

260
00:10:05,501 --> 00:10:09,213
The Israeli army uses heavily armored versions

261
00:10:09,213 --> 00:10:11,312
of the Caterpillar D nine,

262
00:10:11,312 --> 00:10:13,168
with bulletproof glass,

263
00:10:13,168 --> 00:10:16,396
armor plates and even cage armor against

264
00:10:16,396 --> 00:10:17,041
rockets.

265
00:10:17,392 --> 00:10:20,899
Armies around the world use armored dozers

266
00:10:20,899 --> 00:10:23,989
to clear the path for other vehicles,

267
00:10:23,989 --> 00:10:26,076
push away roadside bombs,

268
00:10:26,076 --> 00:10:29,083
and build protective walls of earth.

269
00:10:29,263 --> 00:10:31,336
The armor can add many tonnes,

270
00:10:31,336 --> 00:10:33,202
but the D nine is heavy and

271
00:10:33,202 --> 00:10:35,137
powerful enough to carry it.

272
00:10:35,488 --> 00:10:38,623
It shows how useful the basic machine

273
00:10:38,623 --> 00:10:38,878
is.

274
00:10:39,058 --> 00:10:42,154
A civilian dozer and a military one

275
00:10:42,154 --> 00:10:44,189
push dirt the same way.

276
00:10:44,369 --> 00:10:46,456
The only difference is what they need

277
00:10:46,456 --> 00:10:47,922
to survive while doing it.

278
00:10:48,272 --> 00:10:51,305
Why do bulldozers working in landfills look

279
00:10:51,305 --> 00:10:52,010
different?

280
00:10:52,190 --> 00:10:55,006
Because trash is a terrible surface to

281
00:10:55,006 --> 00:10:55,673
drive on.

282
00:10:55,853 --> 00:10:56,685
It is soft,

283
00:10:56,685 --> 00:10:58,879
full of sharp metal and wire,

284
00:10:58,879 --> 00:11:01,603
and packed with things that can wrap

285
00:11:01,603 --> 00:11:02,738
around rollers.

286
00:11:02,918 --> 00:11:06,144
Landfill dozers get special guards around the

287
00:11:06,144 --> 00:11:08,151
undercarriage, sealed parts,

288
00:11:08,151 --> 00:11:10,589
and screens that keep trash out of

289
00:11:10,589 --> 00:11:11,521
the radiator.

290
00:11:11,871 --> 00:11:14,310
Their main job is to spread and

291
00:11:14,310 --> 00:11:15,726
compact the waste,

292
00:11:15,726 --> 00:11:18,480
crushing it into thin layers so the

293
00:11:18,480 --> 00:11:21,391
landfill holds more and settles less.

294
00:11:21,571 --> 00:11:25,532
Some landfills use compactors with steel wheels

295
00:11:25,532 --> 00:11:26,712
and big teeth,

296
00:11:26,712 --> 00:11:29,747
but tracked dozers are still used to

297
00:11:29,747 --> 00:11:31,938
push and spread the trash.

298
00:11:32,289 --> 00:11:34,899
Landfill space is expensive,

299
00:11:34,899 --> 00:11:38,722
and every cubic meter saved by compaction

300
00:11:38,722 --> 00:11:41,333
means the site lasts longer.

301
00:11:41,513 --> 00:11:44,013
So the machine driving across your old

302
00:11:44,013 --> 00:11:45,855
trash is not just moving it.

303
00:11:46,035 --> 00:11:48,167
It is squeezing years of extra life

304
00:11:48,167 --> 00:11:49,994
out of the hole in the ground.

305
00:11:50,344 --> 00:11:53,356
Why are bulldozers and so many construction

306
00:11:53,356 --> 00:11:54,477
machines yellow?

307
00:11:54,657 --> 00:11:57,420
Because yellow is easy to see.

308
00:11:57,600 --> 00:11:59,542
In late nineteen thirty one,

309
00:11:59,542 --> 00:12:02,732
Caterpillar switched its machines from gray to

310
00:12:02,732 --> 00:12:05,506
a bright color it called Highway Yellow.

311
00:12:05,686 --> 00:12:08,321
Machines were starting to work on roads

312
00:12:08,321 --> 00:12:10,821
with traffic, and a bright color made

313
00:12:10,821 --> 00:12:13,185
them more visible to drivers and to

314
00:12:13,185 --> 00:12:14,266
workers on site.

315
00:12:14,616 --> 00:12:17,665
It also stands out against dirt,

316
00:12:17,665 --> 00:12:19,190
rock and forest,

317
00:12:19,190 --> 00:12:21,953
at dawn, at dusk and in dust.

318
00:12:22,133 --> 00:12:25,454
Other makers followed with yellow or orange,

319
00:12:25,454 --> 00:12:27,868
and today the color is so common

320
00:12:27,868 --> 00:12:30,887
that people call it construction yellow.

321
00:12:31,067 --> 00:12:33,260
Some brands chose orange,

322
00:12:33,260 --> 00:12:35,891
red or green to stand out from

323
00:12:35,891 --> 00:12:39,925
Caterpillar, but bright colors won everywhere.

324
00:12:40,276 --> 00:12:43,699
A simple color choice became the uniform

325
00:12:43,699 --> 00:12:45,581
of an entire industry.

326
00:12:45,761 --> 00:12:48,209
When kids draw a bulldozer,

327
00:12:48,209 --> 00:12:50,022
they draw it yellow.

328
00:12:50,202 --> 00:12:51,969
And it all started as a way

329
00:12:51,969 --> 00:12:54,193
to keep people from getting hit by

330
00:12:54,193 --> 00:12:55,044
the machines.

331
00:12:55,394 --> 00:12:57,576
So here are the eleven.

332
00:12:57,756 --> 00:12:59,902
A claw on the back that tears

333
00:12:59,902 --> 00:13:02,196
the ground, with a tip that can

334
00:13:02,196 --> 00:13:03,898
be replaced in minutes.

335
00:13:04,078 --> 00:13:06,950
A raised sprocket that keeps the gears

336
00:13:06,950 --> 00:13:08,159
out of the dirt,

337
00:13:08,159 --> 00:13:10,804
a curved blade that rolls the load,

338
00:13:10,804 --> 00:13:12,391
and tracks that grip.

339
00:13:12,571 --> 00:13:14,916
A cage around the operator,

340
00:13:14,916 --> 00:13:17,000
a giant built for mines,

341
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,040
a dozer that pushes other machines,

342
00:13:20,040 --> 00:13:21,256
armor for war,

343
00:13:21,256 --> 00:13:23,427
special tracks for trash,

344
00:13:23,427 --> 00:13:25,945
and a yellow coat to be seen.

345
00:13:26,296 --> 00:13:28,834
Next time you pass a construction site,

346
00:13:28,834 --> 00:13:31,048
look at the back of the bulldozer,

347
00:13:31,048 --> 00:13:33,131
and you will know what that claw

348
00:13:33,131 --> 00:13:33,587
is for.

349
00:13:33,767 --> 00:13:36,607
If you want more machines explained part

350
00:13:36,607 --> 00:13:39,305
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

351
00:13:39,485 --> 00:13:41,465
And tell us in the comments which

352
00:13:41,465 --> 00:13:43,445
machine you want us to take apart

353
00:13:43,445 --> 00:13:43,745
next.
